00:39:18,440 --> 00:39:23,160
 So let's say the parameter will be a one,

793
00:39:23,160 --> 00:39:27,000
 a two, a three, and so on.

794
00:39:27,000 --> 00:39:30,280
 If there are, for example, a n features.

795
00:39:30,280 --> 00:39:33,960
 So if there are n dimensional features available to you,

796
00:39:33,960 --> 00:39:37,360
 there will be this x which will be represented

797
00:39:37,360 --> 00:39:40,080
 by this n number of features.

798
00:39:40,080 --> 00:39:41,120
 Why this is important?

799
00:39:41,120 --> 00:39:43,320
 Because in real life, you will not end up having only

800
00:39:43,320 --> 00:39:46,720
 two features, but we'll end up with multiple features

801
00:39:46,720 --> 00:39:49,360
 for a single data point.

802
00:39:49,360 --> 00:39:50,440
 So it is very easy.

803
00:39:50,440 --> 00:39:53,680
 As you can understand, we had this x values and y values

804
00:39:53,680 --> 00:39:54,560
 together.

805
00:39:54,560 --> 00:39:56,800
 And once we had this x value and y values,

806
00:39:56,800 --> 00:39:59,640
 we zipped them together into a single list.

807
00:39:59,640 --> 00:40:04,080
 And we can do the same thing for all type of dimensions.

808
00:40:04,080 --> 00:40:06,640
 If the dimensions are increased in real life,

809
00:40:06,640 --> 00:40:10,160
 we can work on that also, right?

810
00:40:10,160 --> 00:40:13,840
 So we were talking about overlapping clusters previously.

811
00:40:13,840 --> 00:40:16,120
 So when the CELOTI coefficient becomes zero,

812
00:40:16,120 --> 00:40:18,560
 that means the clusters are overlapping.

813
00:40:18,560 --> 00:40:21,400
 Now K means algorithm works by selecting

814
00:40:21,400 --> 00:40:22,720
 the different means.

815
00:40:22,720 --> 00:40:25,640
 So for example, if we have selected the mean in this

816
00:40:25,640 --> 00:40:28,640
 position, this position, particular position,

817
00:40:28,640 --> 00:40:32,520
 and we selected another mean, let's say a blue mean

818
00:40:32,520 --> 00:40:34,360
 in this position.

819
00:40:34,360 --> 00:40:37,080
 So generally, the class will be determined

820
00:40:37,080 --> 00:40:43,240
 by this green class, which will be represented like this,

821
00:40:43,240 --> 00:40:47,400
 and the blue class, which will be represented like this.

822
00:40:47,400 --> 00:40:51,040
 So basically, these are kind of like circular kind

823
00:40:51,040 --> 00:40:54,200
 of representations that actually distinguishes

824
00:40:54,200 --> 00:40:59,000
 between the different groups that you are going to cluster.

825
00:40:59,000 --> 00:41:03,320
 But you can understand if we have a data point.

826
00:41:03,320 --> 00:41:12,560
 So if we consider a data point, let's say

827
00:41:12,560 --> 00:41:22,120
 we have this data point where we have this x here and this O

828
00:41:22,120 --> 00:41:29,480
 here, then how is it possible for you to cluster?

829
00:41:29,480 --> 00:41:31,360
 Because there will be two centroids,

830
00:41:31,360 --> 00:41:33,680
 because we have selected the value of K equals to 2.

831
00:41:33,680 --> 00:41:37,960
 And you can see that the centroids will lie somewhere here.

832
00:41:37,960 --> 00:41:40,600
 The two different centroids will lie somewhere here.

833
00:41:40,600 --> 00:41:42,560
 So let's consider these two centroids.

834
00:41:42,560 --> 00:41:44,960
 One is pink, one is blue.

835
00:41:44,960 --> 00:41:47,680
 And they will not be able to cluster this properly.

836
00:41:47,680 --> 00:41:50,280
 They will not be able to cluster this properly,

837
00:41:50,280 --> 00:41:52,760
 because their centroids are same.

838
00:41:52,760 --> 00:41:55,800
 This is the centroid-based algorithm.

839
00:41:55,800 --> 00:41:59,520
 So here, the clustering algorithm will only

840
00:41:59,520 --> 00:42:03,680
 work if the clusters are non-overlapping.

841
00:42:03,680 --> 00:42:05,160
 So this is the idea of overlapping.

842
00:42:05,160 --> 00:42:08,680
 So you don't want these means to be together.

843
00:42:08,680 --> 00:42:10,680
 You want these means to be separated,

844
00:42:10,680 --> 00:42:12,600
 just like the previous case or the example

845
00:42:12,600 --> 00:42:15,120
 that we were already observing.

846
00:42:15,120 --> 00:42:17,880
 So if this kind of data set we are having,

847
00:42:17,880 --> 00:42:20,440
 then K means clustering works wonders.

848
00:42:20,440 --> 00:42:23,800
 So this is how you can understand which particular algorithm

849
00:42:23,800 --> 00:42:26,080
 you can select for a particular data set,

850
00:42:26,080 --> 00:42:30,120
 if you know the idea behind how the model works.

851
00:42:30,120 --> 00:42:32,400
 Now again, let's go to the code.

852
00:42:32,400 --> 00:42:37,360
 So we have seen till now that by changing the value of K,

853
00:42:37,360 --> 00:42:40,960
 we can understand using different evaluation metrics

854
00:42:40,960 --> 00:42:45,160
 that which particular value of K we can be selecting.

855
00:42:45,160 --> 00:42:49,080
 But that is a very inefficient way of selecting the value of K.

856
00:42:49,080 --> 00:42:51,360
 Instead of that, there is another way

857
00:42:51,360 --> 00:42:55,840
 that we can use to actually approximate the value of K.

858
00:42:55,840 --> 00:42:58,200
 This we don't use regularly.

859
00:42:58,200 --> 00:43:00,480
 It is not guaranteed to give you good value of K

860
00:43:00,480 --> 00:43:02,800
 or correct value of K, regardless,

861
00:43:02,800 --> 00:43:05,080
 that gives us an understanding of where

862
00:43:05,080 --> 00:43:07,840
 the particular value of K may lie.

863
00:43:07,840 --> 00:43:10,600
 So for that, we need to use something

864
00:43:10,600 --> 00:43:13,000
 known as the ELBO method.

865
00:43:13,000 --> 00:43:16,360
 So we will see that how this particular thing works

866
00:43:16,360 --> 00:43:23,280
 is that from the cluster we are again initializing this K means.

867
00:43:23,280 --> 00:43:27,720
 And in this data, we are again taking

868
00:43:27,720 --> 00:43:31,680
 the list of this X and Y, which is again the same data

869
00:43:31,680 --> 00:43:33,000
 that we have already used.

870
00:43:33,000 --> 00:43:34,720
 There's nothing new here.

871
00:43:34,720 --> 00:43:37,160
 And then there is another list that we

872
00:43:37,160 --> 00:43:40,120
 are creating, which is an empty list for now.

873
00:43:40,120 --> 00:43:44,120
 And it will capture the sum of squared errors

874
00:43:44,120 --> 00:43:46,280
 for all these clusters.

875
00:43:46,280 --> 00:43:49,000
 So sum of squared errors are nothing but the errors,

876
00:43:49,000 --> 00:43:54,480
 basically the distance from the actual cluster center.

877
00:43:54,480 --> 00:43:55,880
 So if the centroid is known to you,

878
00:43:55,880 --> 00:43:58,200
 you can just calculate its error.

879
00:43:58,200 --> 00:44:01,040
 But this thing is very, very easy to understand

880
00:44:01,040 --> 00:44:04,080
 if you have gone through this algorithm theory aspect.

881
00:44:04,080 --> 00:44:06,960
 So basically, we are getting the sum of squared errors

882
00:44:06,960 --> 00:44:09,480
 or within cluster sum of squared errors, whatever

883
00:44:09,480 --> 00:44:10,720
 may be the case.

884
00:44:10,720 --> 00:44:17,080
 And now we are having this loop for all the 11 times.

885
00:44:17,080 --> 00:44:19,120
 So what is this 1 to 11?

886
00:44:19,120 --> 00:44:21,680
 It's basically how many number of times

887
00:44:21,680 --> 00:44:23,880
 we are going to check for the value of K.

888
00:44:23,880 --> 00:44:26,280
 So this is nothing but the value of K.

889
00:44:26,280 --> 00:44:31,280
 So till like 1 to 11, we are going to check.

890
00:44:31,280 --> 00:44:33,560
 I think this is quite easy to understand.

891
00:44:33,560 --> 00:44:36,040
 So these are the number of values of K

892
00:44:36,040 --> 00:44:37,200
 that we are going to check.

893
00:44:37,200 --> 00:44:38,680
 And then from that value, we are going

894
00:44:38,680 --> 00:44:42,440
 to select a particular value of K that is a good value.

895
00:44:42,440 --> 00:44:44,920
 Now how do we know that a particular value is good?

896
00:44:44,920 --> 00:44:46,720
 Let's see that.

897
00:44:46,720 --> 00:44:49,560
 So here we are again initializing the K-means clustering

898
00:44:49,560 --> 00:44:50,400
 algorithm.

899
00:44:50,400 --> 00:44:55,160
 But now the n clusters will be initialized by i.

900
00:44:55,160 --> 00:44:55,680
 What is i?

901
00:44:55,680 --> 00:45:01,280
 It is the iterate that is ranging between 1 and 11.

902
00:45:01,280 --> 00:45:03,280
 And n in it is again auto.

903
00:45:03,280 --> 00:45:06,520
 We are not going to change that parameter at all for now.

904
00:45:06,520 --> 00:45:12,360
 So this is how we are setting up our algorithm.

905
00:45:12,360 --> 00:45:15,200
 We are then going to fit the data that we have created.

906
00:45:15,200 --> 00:45:17,720
 This is the exact same data that we were working on.

907
00:45:17,720 --> 00:45:20,600
 And the K-means.fit actually does it for you.

908
00:45:20,600 --> 00:45:24,640
 And then we're going to take the error from the model.

909
00:45:24,640 --> 00:45:26,640
 How we are going to take the error from the model?

910
00:45:26,640 --> 00:45:29,240
 We are going to take the error by appending this

911
00:45:29,240 --> 00:45:30,600
 to the inertia.

912
00:45:30,600 --> 00:45:34,800
 And the error is basically K-means.inertia.

913
00:45:34,800 --> 00:45:39,000
 So this will give you an error value for the model.

914
00:45:39,000 --> 00:45:41,600
 This is something that the model calculates by itself.

915
00:45:41,600 --> 00:45:45,520
 This is a sum of squared error that model is optimizing for.

916
00:45:45,520 --> 00:45:48,720
 So once you have this, we can store this

917
00:45:48,720 --> 00:45:51,040
 in this inertia list.

918
00:45:51,040 --> 00:45:54,040
 So this inertia list is being appended

919
00:45:54,040 --> 00:45:59,360
 by all the errors for clustering value of K equals to 1,

920
00:45:59,360 --> 00:46:04,200
 K equals to 2, K equals to 3, K equals to 4, and so on.

921
00:46:04,200 --> 00:46:05,320
 So there is one thing.

922
00:46:05,320 --> 00:46:06,920
 Like if the value of K equals to 1,

923
00:46:06,920 --> 00:46:07,960
 what is going to happen?

924
00:46:07,960 --> 00:46:08,880
 What is going to happen?

925
00:46:08,880 --> 00:46:11,120
 We can see that all.

926
00:46:11,120 --> 00:46:13,480
 So we are just going to print the inertials for now.

927
00:46:13,480 --> 00:46:16,240
 Inertials are the error.

928
00:46:16,240 --> 00:46:21,520
 So we see that we get a set of values for inertia.

929
00:46:21,520 --> 00:46:24,080
 So these are the set of inertial values

930
00:46:24,080 --> 00:46:26,480
 for basically all the errors.

931
00:46:26,480 --> 00:46:29,160
 Now what we are going to do is we are going to plot this.

932
00:46:29,160 --> 00:46:31,880
 It's very, very easy to plot this list.

933
00:46:31,880 --> 00:46:35,160
 So we are going to plot between the range of 1 and 11

934
00:46:35,160 --> 00:46:38,240
 because we are having this 11 points, 11 Ks.

935
00:46:38,240 --> 00:46:40,640
 And we are having this, basically 10 Ks.

936
00:46:40,640 --> 00:46:43,640
 And we are having this inertial, which is the error.

937
00:46:43,640 --> 00:46:46,160
 And we are giving a marker O so that this is just

938
00:46:46,160 --> 00:46:48,320
 for visualization.

939
00:46:48,320 --> 00:46:51,240
 We are giving the title and the x label and the y label.

940
00:46:51,240 --> 00:46:53,440
 So x label here is the number of clusters,

941
00:46:53,440 --> 00:46:56,320
 which is determined by the value of K.

942
00:46:56,320 --> 00:46:59,400
 And this y label is basically the sum of squared errors.

943
00:47:01,920 --> 00:47:04,440
 Now we can just run this code.

944
00:47:04,440 --> 00:47:06,880
 And we'll see that we'll end up with a graph

945
00:47:06,880 --> 00:47:11,040
 that looks like this, the graph that looks like this.

946
00:47:11,040 --> 00:47:14,440
 So for one, it is very, very high, right?

947
00:47:14,440 --> 00:47:16,520
 One, it is very, very high.

948
00:47:16,520 --> 00:47:18,720
 The within cluster sum of squared errors.

949
00:47:18,720 --> 00:47:22,760
 But as we go on this side, it is getting less.

950
00:47:22,760 --> 00:47:24,280
 So the error is getting less.

951
00:47:24,280 --> 00:47:29,000
 Now we will see that a lesser number of error is good, right?

952
00:47:29,000 --> 00:47:33,000
 But in reality, like a less error doesn't mean good.

953
00:47:33,000 --> 00:47:35,600
 Because these are clustering algorithms.

954
00:47:35,600 --> 00:47:40,240
 The errors will go on getting less if you increase

955
00:47:40,240 --> 00:47:41,760
 the number of clusters.

956
00:47:41,760 --> 00:47:44,000
 Because those will try to overfit.

957
00:47:44,000 --> 00:47:47,480
 If you know the term overfit, you are trying to fit

958
00:47:47,480 --> 00:47:50,280
 most of the data points, right?

959
00:47:50,280 --> 00:47:53,760
 So for example, you have 10 data points

960
00:47:53,760 --> 00:47:56,120
 and you have 10 clusters.

961
00:47:56,120 --> 00:47:59,080
 Each cluster will be represented by one group.

962
00:47:59,080 --> 00:48:00,880
 It will be highly overfitting.

963
00:48:00,880 --> 00:48:05,200
 But there you will see that the error will be very, very less.

964
00:48:05,200 --> 00:48:08,600
 But we don't want error to be the least.

965
00:48:08,600 --> 00:48:12,680
 But rather, we want a portion of the error

966
00:48:12,680 --> 00:48:16,960
 where we see a good change in the error.

967
00:48:16,960 --> 00:48:21,320
 So what we are observing here is the change in error.

968
00:48:21,320 --> 00:48:26,600
 Here we are seeing the change in within cluster sum of squared errors.

969
00:48:26,600 --> 00:48:32,000
 So here you see that we found an increasing value

970
00:48:32,000 --> 00:48:35,960
 at this stage of 2, where the value of k equals 2.

971
00:48:35,960 --> 00:48:37,680
 Before that, it is very high.

972
00:48:37,680 --> 00:48:42,600
 And then it's dipping in a very low fashion.

973
00:48:42,600 --> 00:48:46,920
 So this value of 2 can be selected as the value of k

974
00:48:46,920 --> 00:48:49,040
 in our algorithm.

975
00:48:49,040 --> 00:48:52,400
 So this is how we can determine the k value.

976
00:48:52,400 --> 00:48:55,280
 And this method is known as the ELBO method.

977
00:48:55,280 --> 00:48:57,480
 Because we are trying to find an ELBO

978
00:48:57,480 --> 00:49:04,200
 where we see a maximum deviation of this error, right?

979
00:49:04,200 --> 00:49:06,960
 So this ELBO method, as I told you,

980
00:49:06,960 --> 00:49:08,720
 can be used to find the value of k,

981
00:49:08,720 --> 00:49:13,280
 but it's not that much reliable, but can be used in many cases.

982
00:49:13,280 --> 00:49:14,720
 To actually give you a better idea,

983
00:49:14,720 --> 00:49:19,520
 like which out of maybe five values which I can select.

984
00:49:27,320 --> 00:49:32,160
 Now let's just change this to the k value of 2 and 11.

985
00:49:32,160 --> 00:49:34,680
 At the same code, nothing is changed.

986
00:49:34,680 --> 00:49:37,000
 Only the change is that we are going

987
00:49:37,000 --> 00:49:39,720
 to see that if we can use the evaluation

988
00:49:39,720 --> 00:49:41,360
 matrix that we have used earlier.

989
00:49:41,360 --> 00:49:44,160
 Because we were seeing that if we change the value of k,

990
00:49:44,160 --> 00:49:46,440
 the evaluation matrix also changed.

991
00:49:46,440 --> 00:49:49,080
 And the evaluation matrix are nothing but actually calculating

992
00:49:49,080 --> 00:49:51,160
 the error by itself.

993
00:49:51,160 --> 00:49:53,680
 There is the sum of squared error from the model

994
00:49:53,680 --> 00:49:54,560
 that you get.

995
00:49:54,560 --> 00:49:56,920
 And here we are giving, for example,

996
00:49:56,920 --> 00:49:59,440
 Euclidean distance to measure this error function,

997
00:49:59,440 --> 00:50:00,840
 or error distance.

998
00:50:00,840 --> 00:50:03,280
 So here, for example, for the value

999
00:50:03,280 --> 00:50:08,160
 of i in the range of 2 and 11, we

1000
00:50:08,160 --> 00:50:11,680
 find that if we get these kind of values where

1001
00:50:11,680 --> 00:50:16,560
 we have this 2, 11, and now instead of giving the error

1002
00:50:16,560 --> 00:50:19,480
 values as inertials that we earlier did,

1003
00:50:19,480 --> 00:50:24,440
 instead of that we are just giving the values of CELOTI score.

1004
00:50:24,440 --> 00:50:28,200
 We are directly giving the CELOTI score to this inertia.

1005
00:50:28,200 --> 00:50:30,960
 We're just storing it.

1006
00:50:30,960 --> 00:50:33,560
 And CELOTI score, as we have already discussed,

1007
00:50:33,560 --> 00:50:36,440
 that it takes data, the C parameter,

1008
00:50:36,440 --> 00:50:39,320
 which is basically the different classes or clusters,

1009
00:50:39,320 --> 00:50:41,880
 and this metric, which is the distance that it

1010
00:50:41,880 --> 00:50:45,200
 is going to calculate there.

1011
00:50:45,200 --> 00:50:48,760
 And then we are going to print this inertia again just to see.

1012
00:50:48,760 --> 00:50:51,240
 So we have this value.

1013
00:50:51,240 --> 00:50:53,840
 Now again, plot this.

1014
00:50:53,840 --> 00:50:55,000
 I'm not explaining the code.

1015
00:50:55,000 --> 00:50:56,320
 It's the same code.

1016
00:50:56,320 --> 00:50:57,760
 Nothing changed.

1017
00:50:57,760 --> 00:51:04,480
 So here also we will see that if we take this,

1018
00:51:04,480 --> 00:51:09,320
 we get the highest amount of value in 2.

1019
00:51:09,320 --> 00:51:13,880
 Now you will see that we cannot select inertials from 1.

1020
00:51:13,880 --> 00:51:17,080
 The range will be from 2 because the clustering algorithms

1021
00:51:17,080 --> 00:51:20,440
 always expect that there will be at least two clusters.

1022
00:51:20,440 --> 00:51:25,640
 So you cannot give the value which is less than 2.

1023
00:51:25,640 --> 00:51:30,080
 So here you see that we get the highest value at 7.

1024
00:51:30,080 --> 00:51:33,280
 Now what this CELOTI coefficient tells?

1025
00:51:33,280 --> 00:51:37,480
 It ranges from minus 1 to plus 1.

1026
00:51:37,480 --> 00:51:40,720
 Plus 1 is the best score that you can get, anything

1027
00:51:40,720 --> 00:51:42,280
 that is near to plus 1.

1028
00:51:42,280 --> 00:51:45,320
 Minus 1 is the worst case that you can get.

1029
00:51:45,320 --> 00:51:47,760
 And around 0 it is an overlapping cluster,

1030
00:51:47,760 --> 00:51:52,000
 which is also not good, or that is also what we don't want.

1031
00:51:52,000 --> 00:51:55,200
 So here you see that we directly get,

1032
00:51:55,200 --> 00:51:58,080
 by using CELOTI score, a similar kind of thing

1033
00:51:58,080 --> 00:52:00,120
 that we get from an ELBO method.

1034
00:52:00,120 --> 00:52:02,000
 But here we are not finding the ELBO.

1035
00:52:02,000 --> 00:52:07,200
 We are finding the maximum value, which is more than 0.7.

1036
00:52:07,200 --> 00:52:13,080
 So maybe 0.7, 0.73 or something like that.

1037
00:52:13,080 --> 00:52:16,560
 And this represents to the cluster number 2.

1038
00:52:16,560 --> 00:52:19,560
 So using this method also we can see

1039
00:52:19,560 --> 00:52:24,360
 that the cluster that we need to select is determined.

1040
00:52:24,360 --> 00:52:27,600
 So this is also the two clusters.

1041
00:52:27,600 --> 00:52:31,080
 So I think this is the part that we were discussing.

1042
00:52:31,080 --> 00:52:32,400
 This is an important aspect.

1043
00:52:32,400 --> 00:52:33,920
 If you are able to understand this,

1044
00:52:33,920 --> 00:52:36,360
 the next part will be very, very easy.

